// Track-0 MUST-NOT-COMPILE entry (SV1 wave 4) — `refcount-torture.maxon` written
// against SERVICES, which is a COMPILE-TIME REFUSAL, and that is the point.
//
// ⭐⭐ `refcount-torture.maxon` HANDS THE SAME HEAP `String` TO TWELVE TASKS. That
// is what makes it a refcount race: every incref and every decref lands on one
// word, and its header carries the 96-run table that justified making the step a
// plain load/add/store. It stays an `async` program and `pin-matrix.sh` keeps
// asserting `workers=1 steals=0` of it, because an `async` frame is a COROUTINE of
// the green thread that called it and there is no second M to race from.
//
// ⭐⭐ THE SAME PROGRAM CANNOT BE WRITTEN WITH SERVICES, AND THE COMPILER SAYS SO.
// A send is a MOVE: the sending frame gives up the reference it holds and the
// service becomes the box's one owner. So the FIRST send takes `shared` away from
// `main`, and the second one is asking `main` to give up a reference it no longer
// has. Under the `async` shape those two hand-overs are what put twelve green
// threads on twelve Ms stepping one plain refcount word — exactly the shape the
// atomic used to cover and the pin now removes; under a service the shape has no
// spelling at all.
//
// ⚠⚠ **TWO SENDS, WRITTEN OUT, AND NOT A LOOP — MEASURED, BECAUSE THE FIRST CUT OF
// THIS FILE WAS A LOOP AND `pin-matrix.sh` CAUGHT IT.** Handing a value declared
// OUTSIDE a loop to a send INSIDE it is refused earlier and by a different rule:
// `E2015 moving a value declared outside this loop from inside the loop body`,
// which is about path-sensitive elaboration across a back edge and says nothing
// whatever about transferability. A must-not-compile program that fails for the
// wrong reason asserts nothing, which is why the driver checks the CODE and not
// merely that the build failed — and why the mirror of "twelve tasks" here is two
// straight-line sends. The COUNT was never the subject: twelve is what produces
// CONTENTION, and contention cannot arise at all once the shape is refused.
//
// ⇒ **THE REFUSAL IS THE MEASUREMENT.** `refcount-race.sh` discriminates the
// `async` shape by REPEATING it and tabulating exit codes, because the race is
// intermittent and a single run cannot see it. The service shape needs no runs at
// all: it is unrepresentable, and this file is where that fact is kept so it is
// re-checked by a driver rather than remembered. `pin-matrix.sh` builds it and
// asserts the build FAILS with E3102 (`use of moved value`, which is sharper
// than the transferability refusal E3138: the first send did not merely threaten a
// second owner, it TOOK the reference) — a build that starts SUCCEEDING here is the
// transfer rule having quietly stopped covering the shape it was written for.

typealias Integer = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// `refcount-torture`'s round count. It never runs, but a number that differed from
// the program this one mirrors would invite the reader to think the shapes differ
// in some way that matters here. They differ in exactly one way: the send.
let rounds = 6000

type Hammer
	var acc as Integer

	static function create() returns Self
		return Self{acc: 0}
	end 'create'

	// The borrowing handler `service-torture.maxon`'s `take` is, so the loop owes the
	// payload drop — which is the second half of what would race.
	export function churn(s String, perRound Integer)
		self.acc = self.acc + (s.byteLength() * perRound)
	end 'churn'
end 'Hammer'

function main() returns ExitCode
	let first = spawn Hammer.create()
	let second = spawn Hammer.create()

	// ⚠ INTERPOLATION, not a literal, for `service-torture`'s reason: an immortal
	// `.rdata` record takes `__str_retain`'s COPY arm and never touches a refcount, so
	// a literal would be a program with nothing to race and the refusal would be about
	// a different thing.
	let shared = "a shared payload {rounds}"

	// This one is legal and is the whole of what a service may be handed: `main` owns
	// `shared` alone, gives up its reference, and `first` becomes the box's one owner.
	first.churn(shared, perRound: rounds)

	// ⛔ THE LINE THAT MUST NOT COMPILE. `main` no longer holds a reference to give up,
	// and the only way this could be made to work is the one the language refuses —
	// two green threads owning one box and stepping one plain refcount word.
	second.churn(shared, perRound: rounds)

	return 42
end 'main'
